<!--
  File:     F100-PW-229.xml
  Author:   Aero-Matic v 0.8

  Inputs:
    name:           F100
    type:           turbine
    thrust:         17800 lb
    augmented?      yes
    injected?       no

  intercept_sim: the Mil/AugThrust tables are FITTED to the F-16C flight
  manual, T.O. GR1F-16CJ-1-1 (Hellenic AF, 2003) Appendix A, F100-PW-229,
  "data basis flight test": its MIL and MAX AB level-acceleration tables
  at drag index 0, sea level-30 000 ft (MIL) and -40 000 ft (MAX AB). With
  the model's own drag and alpha (tools/drivers/drag_sample), thrust at each Mach
  row is chosen so that (W/g) dV/dt = T cos(alpha) - D, with weight falling
  by the manual's fuel, reproduces the manual's times; generated by
  tools/fit/thrust_fit.py (its write option); do not edit by hand. MIL values are
  what JSBSim's FGTurbine needs to produce the fitted thrust given the
  IdleThrust table: T = milthrust * (idle + (1 - idle) * MilThrust).

  They replace Aero-Matic's generic augmented-turbofan lapse (written
  identically for every afterburning engine), which gave 35-50% too much
  excess power subsonic at 40 000 ft, 40-60% too little at Mach 1.45-1.75,
  and ended in a 60 000 ft column of zeros that halved thrust by 55 000 ft.
  Its shape is still used where the data do not reach: Mach rows outside
  each altitude's data follow the next higher altitude whose data reach
  further (else the generic shape), and 10 000 ft below sea level keeps the
  generic ratio to sea level.

  Above the accelerations (40 000 ft AB, 30 000 ft MIL) there are no
  engine data. MIL at 40 000 ft is fitted to the manual's 500 ft/min climb
  envelope (Fig. A8-2, Ps at 1 g); above that, thrust scales with pressure
  delta (corrected thrust constant in the isothermal stratosphere). AB
  scales as delta^1.25 above 40 000 ft: augmentor combustion efficiency
  falls with burner pressure; the exponent is calibrated to the combat
  ceiling chart (Fig. A8-1), see thrust_fit.json. IdleThrust is its
  sea-level row scaled with delta: Aero-Matic's rose with altitude to more
  than the whole military thrust at 50 000 ft. All three tables are written
  by tools/fit/thrust_fit.py; do not edit by hand. Fitted static sea-level AB
  thrust is 27 713 lbf against the 29 100 lbf uninstalled rating.
  AugThrust has extra rows at Mach 1.7, 1.9 and 2.1, where the data's Ps
  falls steeply. See docs/VALIDATION.md and docs/REFERENCE.md 4.1.

  Fuel flow is thrust x TSFC (military) or x ATSFC (afterburner). Both are
  FITTED by tools/fit/fuel_fit.py (its write option, run after thrust_fit.py
  and tools/drivers/cruise_sample); do not edit by hand. ATSFC = c0 theta^p
  (1 + c1 Mach), theta the static temperature ratio, to the flight manual's
  fuel-flow chart, Fig. A8-4. Military TSFC is that form at full power,
  fitted to the chart, times a part-throttle factor in x = 1 - N2norm
  fitted to the manual's steady Subsonic Cruise tables (every gross weight
  but 24 000 lb, which validation flies); the factor is 1 at full military
  power. The cruise points carry the model's drag and the manual cannot
  separate the two, so the factor is an effective part-throttle TSFC that
  absorbs any drag error at CL 0.2-0.5 (docs/REFERENCE.md, Fuel flow).
  JSBSim limits how fast fuel flow follows thrust (1 000 pph/s military,
  5 000 pph/s afterburning), so burned fuel lags a throttle step.
-->

